Heterogeneous cryo-EM involves non-rigid 3D reconstruction from noisy 2D images. 
For such an inverse problem, regularization and inductive bias are key.  Local smoothness is a natural choice for regularization, however, smoothness alone is not sufficient as nearby regions can deform in somewhat independent ways~\cite{3DFlex-NM-2023}. 
Further, the presence or absence of biomolecule parts is not dictated by spatial proximity alone.
Macromolecular complexes, like many objects, naturally possess a part-based structure that connects to their compositional and conformational variations.
But a coherent 3D part-decomposition is unavailable \emph{a priori}, and estimating parts from noisy 2D observations is inherently challenging. 
As a consequence, prior work resort to manually designed masks or meshes~\cite{nakane2020multi,3DFlex-NM-2023}.

