\textit{Channel Fusion} 
%\paragraph{Early and mid trajectory conditioning modules}
%Since GLC follows an encoder-decoder scheme, we experiment with two modules for injecting the input gaze trajectories into the model for the prediction of the output gaze trajectory. 
The channel fusion module (Figure~\ref{fig:approccio_2}(1)) adds the heatmaps in $Q_{1:F/2}$ to the first $F/2$ frames of the input video, $V_{t-F:t-F/2}$ as an additional channel. The values of this channel are set to zero for the remaining frames. This form of early fusion acts as a \textit{soft conditioning} aiming to include information about the input gaze trajectory in the computation. Note that, in order to incorporate information about the relationships between haze and input frames, the model needs to learn how to compute suitable gaze representations from the additional input channel during training.

\noindent
\textit{Correlation Fusion}
This approach (Figure~\ref{fig:approccio_2}(2)) aims to fuse visual tokens with the gaze trajectory token computed by the token embedding layer. 
Inspired by global-local fusion originally proposed in~\cite{lai2022eye}, this is done in two stages. First, within the transformer encoder, where attention between all visual features and the gaze token is computed. In this stage, correlations between all visual tokens and across visual and gaze tokens are leveraged to obtain a strong representation. Second, within the dedicated gaze-visual correlation module. Here, attention is computed only between the gaze token and the visual tokens, thus learning a dedicated attention mechanism which explicitly enriches the representation of each visual token with gaze information. 
%hence providing a much more focused mechanism to condition gaze prediction on the input trajectory. 
Note that this fusion mechanism operates both at the early (through the encoder) and mid (through the gaze-visual correlation module) levels, thus allowing to leverage low-level (co-occurrences of gaze and visual features) and more semantic (co-occurrences of gaze and semantic visual concepts) information.
%The Gaze-Frame Correlation module, Figure~\ref{fig:approccio_2}(2), explicitly models the correlation between gaze information and each local visual token. Specifically, the heatmaps $Q_{1
%/2}$ are encoded and treated as a token. Although gaze information has been explicitly embedded into the visual token with the previous fusion strategy, the transformer encoder treats the gaze and visual tokens equivalently. In this case, the gaze-visual correlation is diluted by correlations among the local visual tokens, limiting its impact. To explicitly model the connection between global and local visual features, we calculate the correlation between each gaze token and the local visual token, as well as its self-correlation.

