\subsubsection{Network Partitioning}
\vspace{-0.1cm}
The first step of MTT is to divide the whole network into stages which will then be set to different time steps for each forwarding process. We denote the setting of time steps of different stages in each forwarding process of MTT by a temporal configuration vector $\bm{t}=(t_1, \dots, t_n)$, where $t_i$ denotes the time step of the $i$-th stage and $n$ is the total number of stages. The forwarding of a partitioned SNN $S_P$ can be denoted by $S_{P}(\bm{x}, \bm{t})$, where $\bm{x}$ is an input and $\bm{t}$ is the temporal configuration vector.
%In TFSNN, the setting of time steps of these stages can be denoted by a temporal configuration vector $\bm{t}=(t_1, ..., t_n)$, where $t_i$ is the time step of the $i$-th stage and $n$ is the total number of stages. The forwarding of a TFSNN can be denoted by $S_{TF}(\bm{x}, \bm{t})$, where $\bm{x}$ is an input and $\bm{t}$ is the temporal configuration vector.

%In a Time-Free Spiking Neural Network (TFSNN), the temporal configuration vector $\bm{t} = (t_1, \dots, t_n)$ denotes the time steps of its stages, where $t_i$ represents the time step of the $i$-th stage and $n$ is the total number of stages. The TFSNN's forwarding can be represented as $S_{TF}(\bm{x}, \bm{t})$, where $\bm{x}$ denotes the input and $\bm{t}$ represents the temporal configuration vector.

\vspace{-0.3cm}
\subsubsection{Temporal Transformation Module}
\vspace{-0.1cm}

%In order to construct a Temporal Flexible SNN(TFSNN), we first have to design a reasonable inter-block communication rule between blocks with different time steps. To achieve this, we propose a plug-and-play module called  temporal transformation module(TTM) .

\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.6\linewidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{pics/TTM_demo.pdf}
    \caption{(A) Downsampling TTM when $t_{in}$=5 and $t_{out}$=3. (B) Upsampling TTM when $t_{in}$=3 and $t_{out}$=5.}
    \label{fig:ttm}
    \vspace{-0.2cm}
\end{wrapfigure}
We then design the inter-block communication rule between adjacent stages with different simulation time steps-temporal transformation module (TTM). When adjacent stages are of the same number of time steps, TTM becomes an identity transformation. Otherwise, TTMs can be categorized into 2 types as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:ttm}-downsampling TTM and upsampling TTM. In downsampling TTMs, we borrow from the pooling layer and divide input time frames into $t_{out}$ groups of adjacent frames. We then sum up the frames within each group to form $t_{out}$ time frames. By contrast, the upsampling type of TTM replicates each input time frame and assigns it to all output frames in the corresponding group, using the grouping policy same as the downsampling type of TTM with $t_{out}$ frames of input and $t_{in}$ frames of output.
Now, the task at hand is to identify a suitable policy to partition $l$ frames into $k$ groups, where $l\ge k$. A natural idea is to group them as evenly as possible to minimize temporal mismatch. According to this idea, we use the following policy shown in Eq. \ref{sp-policy} where $b_i$ denotes the index of the first frame of group $i$. Explanation of our design is in Sec. \ref{apx:ttm}.

%Based on the relationship between the size of the input time step $t_{in}$ and the output time step $t_{out}$, we classify TTMs into two types: downsampling TTMs and upsampling TTMs as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:ttm}. For downsampling TTMs, we borrow from the pooling layer: we divide $t_{in}$ time frames into $t_{out}$ groups, each of which consists of several adjacent time frames, and then sum up the time frames within each group to form $t_{out}$ time frames. For the upsampling TTM, we do the opposite. We group output frames with the same grouping policy as the downsampling TTM with $t_{out}$ frames of input and $t_{in}$ frames of output and directly copy the $i$-th input frame to all output frames of the $i$-th group.



\begin{equation} \label{sp-policy}
    b_{i} = \lfloor \frac {(i - 1) \cdot l} {k} - \varepsilon \rceil + 1, i \in \{1, ..., k\}
\end{equation}

