\paragraph{Maximum likelihood estimation} 
That the negative log likelihood in~\eqref{eq:nll} has the same form of the NLL of a Closed Set Label Shift estimation problem with $K+1$ classes (see Appendix.~\ref{Asec:related-works} or \citealt{alexandari2020maximum}).
%\NB{d?- if here then below - I think you mean closed set} 
Observing this similarity, we can minimize the NLL in~\eqref{eq:nll} by viewing $\Tilde{f}$ as the closed set $K+1$ class classifier and $\Tilde{\vect{c}},\Tilde{\vect{\pi}}$ as parameters of the closed set source and target label distribution. The MLE of target label distribution parameters $\vect{\pi}^{\text{MLE}},\rho_t^{\text{MLE}}$ can be obtained via: 

% Then, the point estimate of $\vect{\pi},\rho_t$ can be obtained via the reparameterisation~\eqref{eq:tilde-pi-and-c}.
%The MLE of target label distribution $\vect{\pi}^{\text{MLE}}$ and target ID data ratio $\rho_t^{\text{MLE}}$ can be obtained via optimizing the NLL in ~\eqref{eq:nll}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:MLE-obj}
    \vect{\pi}^{\text{MLE}},\rho_t^{\text{MLE}} \in \underset{\Tilde{\pi}\in\Delta^K}{\arg\min} - \log L(\vect{\pi},\rho_t;\mathcal{D}^t).
\end{equation}
Although the MLE objective~\eqref{eq:MLE-obj} is not convex in $(\vect{\pi}, \rho_t)$, %it is convex in $\vect{\pi}$ and convex in $\rho_t$. 
we may still derive an EM-algorithm which converges to the global minimum.
Inspired by \citealt{saerens2002adjusting}, we 
%optimize the MLE objective via an EM algorithm and prove that the algorithm converges to 
compute a reparameterised MLE of $\Tilde{\vect{\pi}}$ in ~\eqref{eq:tilde-pi-and-c}. As MLE is invariant under reparameterisation~\cite{murphy2012machine}, this MLE can be mapped back to the MLE of $\vect{\pi}$ and $\rho_t$. 
Details are described in Theorem~\ref{theorem:em-mle}.
% \russ{With our new realisation about invariance of MLE, it is nice to state that ``The NLL in ~\eqref{eq:nll} is non-convex in $(\vect{\pi}, \rho_t)$, but convex in $\vect{\pi}$ and convex in $\rho_t$''. Then state that even though we have non-convexity, our EM algorithm can converge to an MLE through reparameterisation.}
\begin{restatable}{theorem}{propemmle}\textbf{(MLE)}
\label{theorem:em-mle}
    Under Assumption~\ref{assume:A1},~\ref{assume:A2}, the the NLL~\eqref{eq:nll} is convex in $\Tilde{\vect{\pi}}$ (and convex in $\rho_t$), and the EM algorithm MLE-OLS (Alg.~\ref{alg:MAPOLS}) converges to $\vect{\pi}^{\text{MLE}},\rho_t^{\text{MLE}}$~\eqref{eq:MLE-obj}.
\end{restatable}

\paragraph{Maximum a-posteriori estimation}: We may also attempt to compute MAP estimates $\vect{\pi}^{\text{MAP}},\rho_t^{\text{MAP}}$, when prior information about the two parameters are available. 
However the MAP is not invariant under reparameterisations, and the posterior probability density is nonconvex in $(\vect{\pi}, \rho_t)$. 
This makes it difficult to compute MAP estimates, and in this sense the MLE is favourable. 
We detail the use of a Dirichlet prior over ${\vect{\pi}\sim\text{Dir}(K,\vect{\alpha}^{\textbf{in}})}$ and a Beta prior over $\rho_t\sim\text{Beta}(\alpha_1^{\textbf{out}}, \alpha_2^{\textbf{out}})$ in Appendix~\ref{Asubsec:map}.


